----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.  WHAT IT FORBIDS.  The target the hunt set itself (item 123): decide the two footings, 0.082 dex apart, at
    3 sigma, i.e. a total error of 0.027 dex on a_0.  What baryonic calibration does the identity demand?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  canonical : on the M/L-free cut (f_*,loc<0.2, N=129) the TOTAL leverage is 1.213, so a 0.0273 dex a_0 needs the baryonic mass scale to 0.0225 dex = 5.3%
  alt       : on the M/L-free cut (f_*,loc<0.2, N=129) the TOTAL leverage is 1.193, so a 0.0273 dex a_0 needs the baryonic mass scale to 0.0229 dex = 5.4%
  [PASS] K07b.6 THE REQUIREMENT, stated as a bound rather than as a slogan: because the total leverage is bounded BELOW by 1, deciding the two footings at 3 sigma requires the ABSOLUTE baryonic mass scale (helium factor, HI flux calibration, molecular gas, stellar populations -- whichever carries the weight) to better than 6.5% in the best case and about 5.3% on the sample the hunt actually uses, no matter how many galaxies are added. AGAINST MY OWN FIRST DRAFT, which said 'the road is closed': 5-6% on an HI mass scale is at the edge of the state of the art, not orders of magnitude beyond it, so the correct statement is that this is a CALIBRATION problem and provably not a sample-size problem   (need 0.0273 dex in a_0; leverage >= 1 forces the baryonic mass scale to <= 0.0273 dex = 6.5% in the best case (exact deep MOND), and worse everywhere else)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.  THE ESCAPE.  (L1)-(L3) apply to estimators that use the measured NORMALISATION of g_bar. An estimator
    that profiles a per-galaxy baryonic amplitude out of the fit is not covered -- and there the lever is 0.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  k07_shape_marginalised_a0.py does exactly that: Upsilon is fitted per galaxy, and a 4x rescale of the whole
  stellar template returns the identical a_0 (lever +7.6e-13, i.e. zero to machine precision).
  THE PRICE, stated so it is not hidden: profiling out an amplitude costs the deep-MOND information entirely.
  With Upsilon free, deep points constrain only the PRODUCT Upsilon*a_0, so the estimator must live on the
  transition -- which means bright, star-dominated discs, and a sample of 40 rather than 175.
  [PASS] K07b.7 AGAINST INTEREST -- the escape is real but narrow: on the canonical footing most SPARC points are too deep to constrain a_0 once the baryonic amplitude is free, so the marginalised estimator throws away the majority of the data the pointwise estimators use   (54% of SPARC's 3140 points sit at y < 0.3, where Phi < 0.624 and the amplitude-marginalised likelihood is nearly flat in a_0)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.  MUTATION CONTROLS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] MK07b.1 the identity is falsifiable and not a tautology: feeding it the WRONG slope (the deep-MOND value 1/2 at a point where the RAR's actual slope is 0.70) must disagree with the finite-difference lever   (at y=1, f_*=0.5: true Phi = 0.7090 gives -1.2183, matching the numeric -1.2183; the wrong Phi=1/2 gives -0.5000)
  [PASS] MK07b.2 a_0 itself must not appear in the identity except through y -- so changing footing must move the predicted lever, and by the amount the identity says   (a point at g_bar = 1e-11 has lever -0.6828 (canonical) vs -0.6647 (alt))
  [PASS] MK07b.3 with no kernel at all (nu = 1) there is no a_0 to have a lever on -- Phi = 1 exactly, and the identity correctly diverges, which is the right answer rather than a number   (Phi(nu=1) = 1.0000000000; Phi/(1-Phi) -> infinity, i.e. no baryonic calibration whatsoever could pin a_0, which is correct because a_0 is absent from the model)
  [PASS] MK07b.4 the search for a loophole is run and fails: scanning eight decades of y for a point where the total leverage dips below 1 finds none, and the infimum is approached only as y -> 0 where the estimator has no signal left   (minimum total leverage over the 2000 of 2000 y values where Phi is numerically below 1 = 1.001000 at y = 1.00e-06; the excluded points are the Newtonian end, where the leverage is larger, not smaller)


